1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,776
Touch a metal railing and a wooden bench on the same winter morning.

2
00:00:05,276 --> 00:00:07,080
The metal feels freezing.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:09,028
The wood feels almost warm.

4
00:00:09,528 --> 00:00:13,428
But put a thermometer on both, and they read about the same.

5
00:00:14,928 --> 00:00:17,356
So what is your hand actually measuring?

6
00:00:17,856 --> 00:00:21,050
By the end, you'll know why a sauna doesn't burn you,

7
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:26,314
why a metal slide can, and how mint fools the very same sensors.

8
00:00:28,414 --> 00:00:33,070
Leave anything in a room long enough, and it settles at room temperature.

9
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:36,541
Metal, wood, plastic, even the air itself.

10
00:00:37,041 --> 00:00:40,317
Nothing in that room is colder than anything else.

11
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:44,401
Your skin has no thermometer.

12
00:00:45,901 --> 00:00:51,758
It has nerve endings that react to their own temperature, and to how fast it is changing.

13
00:00:52,258 --> 00:00:56,964
One of the main cold sensors is a protein called TRPM8.

14
00:00:57,464 --> 00:01:03,340
When the skin around it cools, it opens, and sends a simple message to your brain: cold.

15
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:11,017
Your hand is warmer than the room, about 34 °C at the surface.

16
00:01:11,517 --> 00:01:15,172
So heat flows out of your skin, into whatever you touch.

17
00:01:16,672 --> 00:01:18,917
How fast depends on the material.

18
00:01:19,417 --> 00:01:25,537
Aluminium conducts heat roughly 1,000 to 2,000 times better than pine wood.

19
00:01:26,037 --> 00:01:29,460
And air conducts about 5 times worse than pine.

20
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:36,814
When two things touch, the place where they meet settles at a temperature in between.

21
00:01:37,314 --> 00:01:40,659
It lands closer to the material that moves heat better.

22
00:01:42,159 --> 00:01:47,849
Touch aluminium at 20 °C, and your skin's surface drops almost to twenty.

23
00:01:48,349 --> 00:01:52,528
Touch pine at 20 °C, and your skin stays around thirty.

24
00:01:53,028 --> 00:01:54,675
Same object temperature.

25
00:01:54,675 --> 00:01:57,009
A very different skin temperature.

26
00:01:58,509 --> 00:02:02,263
And skin temperature is what your cold sensors report.

27
00:02:04,363 --> 00:02:05,756
Now flip it around.

28
00:02:07,256 --> 00:02:12,713
On a hot summer day, a metal slide in full sun can get hot enough to burn skin.

29
00:02:13,213 --> 00:02:17,858
A wooden bench in the same sun feels warm, but rarely dangerous.

30
00:02:18,358 --> 00:02:19,936
Same physics, reversed.

31
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:23,090
The metal pours its heat into your skin.

32
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:25,612
The wood can't deliver it fast enough.

33
00:02:27,712 --> 00:02:29,945
That's also how a hot sauna works.

34
00:02:30,445 --> 00:02:36,499
The air is often 80 to 100 °C, and yet people sit in it for minutes.

35
00:02:37,999 --> 00:02:44,488
Air is a poor conductor, and it holds very little heat, so it delivers heat to your skin slowly.

36
00:02:44,988 --> 00:02:47,633
The benches are wood, for the same reason.

37
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:51,906
But touch a bare metal nail in that bench, and it can burn you.

38
00:02:54,006 --> 00:02:58,390
Your cold sensors can even be fooled with no heat flowing at all.

39
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:06,704
Menthol, the cool part of mint, sticks to TRPM8 and makes it open even at warm skin temperature.

40
00:03:07,204 --> 00:03:10,367
The sensor fires, and your brain hears: cold.

41
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:12,799
Nothing actually got colder.

42
00:03:14,899 --> 00:03:17,208
So your hand is not a thermometer.

43
00:03:17,708 --> 00:03:23,032
It reports what is happening to your own skin: how far it cools, and how fast.

44
00:03:23,532 --> 00:03:25,677
Metal is not colder than wood.

45
00:03:25,677 --> 00:03:28,252
It just pulls your heat away faster.

46
00:03:29,752 --> 00:03:34,579
Next time you grab a cold railing, remember: you're not feeling the cold.

47
00:03:34,579 --> 00:03:37,092
You're feeling your own heat, leaving.

48
00:03:38,592 --> 00:03:41,409
Which surface in your home feels the coldest?

49
00:03:41,409 --> 00:03:42,911
Tell me in the comments.
